From: AW1000_NSS_BYTE
Subject: [PATCH] nss-drv: restore the {} terminator on nss_dt_ids[]

nss_init.c builds its of_device_id table with NSS_DT_SUPPORT=1 on every
kernel newer than 3.4, which is the only configuration this image uses:

    struct of_device_id nss_dt_ids[] = {
        { .compatible = "qcom,nss" },
        { .compatible = "qcom,nss0" },
        { .compatible = "qcom,nss1" },
    };

An of_device_id table must end with an empty entry. modpost checks this and
treats a missing one as an ERROR, not a warning:

    ERROR: modpost: qca-nss-drv: nss_dt_ids[] is not terminated with a NULL entry

The terminator was there in the original source. The 6.12 support patch
(0026-nss-drv-add-support-for-kernel-6.12.patch) removes it in a hunk of its
own, for no stated reason and with no warning that requires it:

    @@ -136,7 +143,6 @@ struct of_device_id nss_dt_ids[] = {
        { .compatible = "qcom,nss1" },
    -   {},
     };

That deletion is why modpost fails. Put the entry back.

This is the third failure in the chain, and the first one that happens after
compiling rather than during it. CI has not seen it: run #18 stopped at
nss_coredump.c, the fourth object out of about two hundred, so the build never
reached modpost.

Verified locally: with the entry restored the modpost error is gone from the
driver's own symbols (the remaining unresolved-symbol warnings in a local build
are every core kernel function, because modules_prepare produces no
Module.symvers; CI builds a real one).
--- a/nss_init.c
+++ b/nss_init.c
@@ -142,8 +142,9 @@
 struct of_device_id nss_dt_ids[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,nss" },
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,nss0" },
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,nss1" },
+	{},
 };
 MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, nss_dt_ids);
 #endif
 
